{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"agent_message","text":"**Verdict: not ready for Architect sign-off. The `:error/kind` question alone should block sign-off until the canonical vocabulary is documented and implemented consistently.** I also found a startup regression and an unmet U4 acceptance criterion.\n\n1. **P1 — The Flutter dao.gui Prototype loses its initial sample frame.**  \n   [terminal.cljc:82](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:82) now binds at `:newest`. The picker calls `start!` before mounting the view; the view’s [LayoutBuilder](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljd/datomworld/demo/dao_gui.cljd:353) emits the sample before constructing the child terminal widget. Consequently, that sample precedes cursor creation and is skipped. This static execution path predicts an empty initial surface until another frame is emitted, contrary to U3’s acceptance criterion. Preserve D4’s `:newest` rule by arranging initial emission after binding, and verify first mount and reopening.\n\n2. **P1 — Terminal stream failures emit signals outside the canonical protocol.**  \n   [terminal.cljc:142](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/postgraphics/terminal.cljc:142) uses raw stream outcomes as `:error/kind`, whereas [dao.postgraphics.md:307](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.postgraphics.md:307) exhaustively permits three ordering-error keywords. Migrating DaoStream does not implicitly version the graphics protocol.\n\n   **Recommendation:** document a terminal-owned extension such as `:dao.terminal/transport-error`, with the underlying stream outcome carried separately. Explicitly define how `:dao.stream/end` maps into it. Also resolve the payload: the current call supplies a **submission counter as `:frame-id`**, although submission and presented-frame identities differ after rejection or skipping—and a transport failure may occur before any frame exists. Update the canonical spec, terminal documentation, implementation, and tests together. Raw outcomes are useful diagnostic detail, but not an acceptable undocumented vocabulary extension.\n\n3. **P1 — U4 still re-reads after terminal input outcomes.**  \n   [event.cljc:826](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/gui/event.cljc:826) returns `:transport-error` without retaining a stopped/error state. Advancing the returned binding calls `next` again. The [new test:259](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/dao/gui/event/bind_test.cljc:259) explicitly asserts that two advances produce two reads.\n\n   This meets “one read per call,” but not U4’s stated “transport-error … does not re-read” criterion. Retain the terminal failure so subsequent advances return without reading, or obtain an explicit architectural revision assigning that responsibility to callers. The current test should not certify the opposite behavior.\n\n4. **P2 — The documented origin guarantee exceeds what lazy cursor creation provides.**  \n   [event.cljc:783](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/gui/event.cljc:783) follows D5 literally by minting on first `advance`. However, creating a binding before input exists does **not** ensure origin observation: bind an empty capacity-one stream, append twice, then advance; minting `:oldest` observes the retained suffix without a gap.\n\n   This is an ambiguity inherited from the plan. Document that callers must supply a cursor minted before production, or perform the first advance before production begins. Add coverage for that boundary. Retaining the minted cursor after a blocked first read is correctly implemented.\n\nThe remaining requested checks:\n\n- **Submission IDs:** the terminal-local counter is appropriate. The canonical spec already describes terminal-local ingress numbering. Repository searches found no production consumer inspecting its numeric value; `dao.gui.event` leaves rejection/skipped accounting signals semantically inert. Tests assert concrete IDs. A gap increments once per observed gap, so these IDs must not be described as counting every evicted frame.\n- **Option compatibility:** confirmed. Both widgets retain public `:signal-stream` and translate it to internal `:signal-handle`. Both artifact callers use `:signal-stream`; no incompatible production caller surfaced.\n- **D4 waiter removal:** confirmed. No stream waiter registration, wake list, or producer-driven presentation remains in the three files. Flutter and browser tickers drive bounded stepping and have disposal/unmount cancellation. Host timer callbacks and presentation functions remain, as D4 explicitly permits.\n- **D5 dispatch and parking:** outcome-map dispatch, pending-output ordering, `full` parking, and dropping failed output writes with a host diagnostic match the disposition. Input gaps return the supplied recovery cursor without adopting it; `recover-input-gap` adopts the caller-provided cursor. Terminal-status and origin qualifications are above.\n- **Artifact split:** independently confirmed structurally in both diffs. The capacity-four frame constructor is separate from runtime-input, signal, output, cursor, and event-driver changes. Namespace imports are shared integration edits. A combined working-tree diff cannot independently establish which author wrote each hunk.\n- **Scripted fixture:** it genuinely exercises refusal followed by admission, ordered flushing, and input transport-error reporting. Reading retained fixture values is non-destructive; changing `:admit` releases parking. No v1 destructive-take mechanism remains. Its no-retry assertion needs correction as described above.\n\nI chose static review and test inspection, accepting your reported three-host suite results without rerunning them. No files were edited. **Manual Flutter/browser animation, pause, drag, and keyboard checks remain open acceptance gates; neither the passing suites nor the abandoned `flutter analyze` substitutes for them.**"}}
